---
title: Install the SDK
description: Keelson's SDKs for TypeScript and Python, and the verifier that checks an Alibi on your machine.
estimate: 3 min
section: SDK
order: 1
---

Two packages do the work: the SDK, which asks for mandates and buys under them, and the verifier, which checks each purchase's Alibi on your machine. Everything runs on the PayPal sandbox and Arbitrum Sepolia, so no real money moves.

| Package | Language | Requires |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `@keelson/sdk` | TypeScript and JavaScript | Node.js 20 or later |
| `@keelson/verifier` | TypeScript and JavaScript, and the `keelson-verify` command | Node.js 20 or later |
| `keelson-finance` (`import keelson`) | Python | Python 3.11 or later; Node.js 20 for `verify_alibi` |

## From the repository

The packages are not on npm or PyPI yet. Until they are, build them from the repository:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/DavidZapataOh/keelson-paypal.git
cd keelson-paypal
pnpm install
pnpm --filter "@keelson/sdk..." --filter "@keelson/verifier..." build
```

Check the verifier against a real Alibi kept in this site:

```sh {% check="sdk-verifier-from-repo" %}
pnpm --filter "@keelson/verifier..." build > /dev/null
node packages/verify/dist/cli.js apps/docs/public/samples/01M6ZWZJHWRRHQEYNJAT7NMSV8.alibi 2> /dev/null | head -1
# expect: ^verified-offline
```

Then sign in and run the quickstart, which asks you for a mandate, buys one pair of shoes at the sandbox store and verifies the Alibi:

{% languages label="Run the quickstart" %}
```sh {% title="TypeScript" %}
node packages/sdk/dist/cli.js login
node packages/sdk/examples/quickstart.mjs
```

```sh {% title="Python" %}
uv run --project packages/sdk-python keelson login
uv run --project packages/sdk-python python packages/sdk-python/examples/quickstart.py
```
{% /languages %}

## What the quickstart does

{% languages label="Quickstart" %}
```ts
import { Keelson, quote } from "@keelson/sdk";
import { verifyAlibi } from "@keelson/verifier";

const keelson = new Keelson(); // signed in with `keelson login`
const req = await keelson.mandates.request({
  name: "Quickstart",
  reason:
    "I buy one pair of Pace Lite running shoes, size 10, at RunFast on the PayPal sandbox, and nothing else.",
  perPurchase: "$100",
  total: "$100",
  until: "1h",
});
console.log(`Approve with your passkey: ${req.approvalUrl}`);
const mandate = await keelson.mandates.waitForApproval(req.id);
const { cart, sources } = await quote([
  { productUrl: "https://store.keelson.finance/products/pace-lite", size: "10", qty: 1 },
]);
const { evidenceRoot } = await keelson.evidence.seal({
  mandateId: mandate.id,
  sources: { ...sources, observation: "Pace Lite, size 10, $99.00." },
});
const { purchaseId } = await keelson.purchases.countersign({ mandate, evidenceRoot, cart });
const purchase = await keelson.purchases.waitFor(purchaseId);
const report = await verifyAlibi(await keelson.alibis.retrieve(purchase.id));
console.log(`Bought ${purchase.id}. Alibi: ${report.verdict}`); // verified-offline
```

```python
from keelson import Keelson, quote, verify_alibi

keelson = Keelson()  # signed in with `keelson login`
why = "I buy one pair of Pace Lite running shoes, size 10, at RunFast on the PayPal sandbox, and nothing else."
req = keelson.mandates.request(name="Quickstart", reason=why, per_purchase="$100", total="$100", until="1h")
print(f"Approve with your passkey: {req.approval_url}")
mandate = keelson.mandates.wait_for_approval(req.id)
q = quote([{"productUrl": "https://store.keelson.finance/products/pace-lite", "size": "10", "qty": 1}])
seen = {**q["sources"], "observation": "Pace Lite, size 10, $99.00."}
sealed = keelson.evidence.seal(mandate_id=mandate.id, sources=seen)
signed = keelson.purchases.countersign(mandate=mandate, evidence_root=sealed["evidenceRoot"], cart=q["cart"])
report = verify_alibi(keelson.alibis.retrieve(keelson.purchases.wait_for(signed.purchase_id).id))
print(f"Bought {signed.purchase_id}. Alibi: {report['verdict']}")  # verified-offline
```
{% /languages %}

Each step has its own page: [authentication](/sdk/authentication/), [mandates](/sdk/mandates/), [purchases](/sdk/purchases/) and [verifying an Alibi](/sdk/verify/). `AsyncKeelson` is the same Python client for asyncio.

## Agent frameworks

The SDKs give a model Keelson's tools under the names and answers of Keelson's MCP server (`keelson_request_mandate`, `keelson_submit_evidence`, `keelson_request_countersign`...). A tool never throws at the model: it answers `{ status, next, message, retryable }`, and `next` says what to do.

{% languages label="Agent frameworks" %}
```ts
import { aiSdkTools } from "@keelson/sdk/ai-sdk"; // Vercel AI SDK
import { openAiAgentsTools } from "@keelson/sdk/openai-agents"; // OpenAI Agents SDK
import { langchainTools } from "@keelson/sdk/langchain"; // LangChain, LangGraph
import { keelsonMcpServer } from "@keelson/sdk/claude-agent-sdk"; // Claude Agent SDK
```

```python
from keelson.adapters.openai_agents import openai_agents_tools
from keelson.adapters.langchain import langchain_tools
from keelson.adapters.claude_agent_sdk import keelson_mcp_server
```
{% /languages %}
